\import{spin-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The real Spin kernel is the scalar pair \citet{Section 1.8, Proposition 1.21, pp. 39--40}{gallier2014clifford}}
\lean/tauceti{TauCeti.CliffordAlgebra.zmodTwoMulEquivKerSpinToSpecialOrthogonal}

\p{For a real quadratic space of signature #{(p,q)}, the kernel of the Spin
action is the scalar pair:

##{(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)_{\mathrm{mult}}
\simeq \ker\bigl(\Spin(p,q)\longrightarrow SO(p,q)\bigr).}

The nontrivial residue class maps to the scalar Clifford element #{-1}.
Thus the two kernel elements are precisely #{1} and #{-1}. This is the kernel
used in Gallier's real double-cover theorem.}

\p{TauCeti proves the analogous algebraic statement over a field #{K}: #{V}
is nontrivial and finite-dimensional, #{Q} is nondegenerate, and #{2} is
invertible in #{K}. Under exactly those hypotheses it constructs the displayed
canonical multiplicative equivalence. This is a generalization of the cited
real result, not an attribution of arbitrary-field kernel classification to
Gallier.}
